  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nUpper level ridging builds today allowing for 925 mb \ntemperatures to climb into the +16 to +18 C range. Clouds have\ncleared out for the majority of the area, with some lingering\nhigh clouds over central Maine. This should allow for good\nmixing, bringing afternoon temperatures into the mid-70s for \nmuch of the area. This is around 20 degrees above normal for \nthis time of year so records at climate sites are going to be \nchallenged. Record values for today can be found in the Climate \nsection below.\n\nClouds and dewpoints increase tonight making for another mild \nnight with temperatures only bottoming out in the mid to upper \n40s in the north, and in the low to mid-50s to the south.\nTemperatures for Thursday will be warm, though not as warm as \ntoday with clouds increasing throughout the day Thursday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA frontal boundary approaches the northeast on Thursday and \nwill be the focus for wetter weather and potentially a soaking \nrain event for at least a portion of our area as it stalls out. \nThe largest point of uncertainty in the forecast is the speed of\nthe front and where it stalls out as it will be the focus for \nan axis of heavy rain as the trough (and eventually an upper \nlow) draw in tropical moisture and drive PWATs into the 1 to 1.5\ninch range. The global model trends have been toward the front \nstalling just offshore which doesn't rule out a soaking rain, \nbut may keep it confined to the coast. The latest probabilities\nof greater than an inch of rain for the Friday through Saturday\ntimeframe currently sit at 50 to 60 percent at the coast and \ndecrease to 20 to 40 percent up into the foothills. Western New\nHampshire and the Western Maine Mountains may miss out on these\nhigher totals, but probabilities of at least half an inch are \n30 to 50 percent for these areas. Of course a slower front and \nfurther west stall changes all of this, so trends need to be \nmonitored closely as the axis of higher precip totals is fairly\nnarrow.\n\nLooking ahead into early next week the pattern remains \nunsettled as troughing persists over the northeast. Chances for\nshowers remain in the forecast through Monday, with upslope \nrain and snow showers potentially persisting into Tuesday.",
